Option: faster-below-the-fold
Name: Draw the home page's sections below the first screen only when they come near (content-visibility: auto), so an old phone gets to a working page sooner
Does: The 15 big sections of the home page below the first screen (visit ... contact) are laid out and painted only when they come near the screen, each starting from a measured height (per width, 360 to 1280, with a small factor for es, vi, hi, zh) that the browser replaces by the real one after the first draw (contain-intrinsic-size: auto). It is switched on by js/guard.js only on a first visit by a link or the address bar: a reload, Back/Forward and any address with # in it are worked out in full, exactly as today, so the places they land on do not move. Print always draws everything. Browsers that do not know content-visibility, and visitors without JavaScript, get today's page. On a slow phone (4x slower CPU, measured) the time until the page is loaded drops by about a third. Known cost: inside these sections text and rounded edges can sit up to one pixel differently from today (paint containment rounds each section's start to a whole pixel); nothing moves and the page height is the same. The sections carry class cv-sec, which the layout-sweep and visual-check tests already switch off. It also adds contain-intrinsic-size to the CSS properties the validity test knows (the test was red with this patch applied).
Answers: d88 (owner question: faster below the fold, an owner choice)
Conflicts: none
Requires: none
Host: any
Order: 70
After: nothing to rebuild; upload again
Strings: none

